E=[
('01-00','Regional cooperation','Regional cooperation joins states around shared development, trade or service goals; SACU and SADC have distinct scope and institutions.','Agreements can widen opportunities but implementation, infrastructure, different capacities and the distribution of gains affect success. An objective is not proof of an achieved result.'),
('01-01','SACU','The Southern African Customs Union links Botswana, Eswatini, Lesotho, Namibia and South Africa in a customs union, including common external tariff arrangements and shared customs revenues.','Its common customs area can facilitate trade and coordination; revenue dependence, unequal productive capacity and administrative barriers still require assessment. It is not the same organisation as SADC.'),
('01-02','SADC','The Southern African Development Community promotes regional integration, development, poverty reduction, peace and security.','Cooperation can connect infrastructure and markets, while capacity differences, implementation gaps and cross-border coordination can limit benefits. Wider membership does not establish uniform rules or outcomes.'),
('02-00','EU cooperation','The European Union negotiates trade arrangements and cooperates with external partners, including development-related agreements.','Market access can support exporters if they meet relevant requirements and can deliver; compliance costs and unequal capacities can limit who benefits. Do not assume an agreement removes every barrier or applies identically to all goods and countries.')]
Q={
1:('Discuss whether a fictional regional agreement alone is sufficient for reliable trade when tariffs are reduced but the delivery bridge remains unusable.','Lower tariffs can improve the trade opportunity, but goods still cannot cross the broken route. Implemented infrastructure and border arrangements are needed; judge actual delivery and shared gains rather than agreement signing alone.',['Judgement: conditional benefit against reliable trade.','Reason: weigh reduced tariff barriers with the remaining physical constraint.']),
2:('Describe this fictional corridor comparison: A has low tariffs but deliveries take ten days with repeated bridge closures; B has similar tariffs and maintained links with four-day delivery.','B combines maintained physical access with delivery six days faster despite similar tariffs. The contrast indicates that tariff arrangements alone do not describe practical market access.',['Evidence: use tariff similarity and delivery times.','Relationship: connect access conditions to the observed contrast without isolating all causes.']),
3:('Discuss a fictional SACU-related proposal for a producer with access to the common customs area but repeated paperwork delays. The aim is dependable delivery; compare more advertising with coordinated document assistance.','Document assistance better addresses the stated administrative barrier. Common customs arrangements provide an opportunity and advertising could expand demand, but neither ensures delivery when paperwork delays persist. Check actual time and cost effects after implementation.',['Judgement: select against dependable delivery.','Reason: distinguish customs opportunity from the documented implementation gap.']),
4:('Discuss a fictional SADC transport project linking regional markets when repair finance is available but states have not agreed maintenance responsibilities.','A connected route may widen markets, but unresolved responsibilities threaten reliability after construction. Agree funded roles and monitor performance before treating the link as a lasting gain; an isolated national upgrade may leave the cross-border bottleneck.',['Judgement: conditional regional benefit.','Reason: weigh network gains against coordination and maintenance risks.']),
5:('Discuss a fictional exporter’s access under an EU-partner agreement when buyers exist but the product fails the required quality standard. Compare promotion alone with feasible training and quality control.','The agreement and buyers create an opportunity, but training and control more directly address the stated access requirement. Promotion alone may attract attention without compliant output. Evaluate the additional costs and retained local gains rather than assuming guaranteed sales.',['Judgement: choose against usable market access.','Reason: weigh agreement/demand with the missing quality capability.']),
6:('Suggest an action where a fictional producer has buyers under a trade arrangement but lacks information on required product documentation. An assistance service is available.','Consult the relevant official requirements and assistance service, then prepare verified documentation. It addresses the information gap without assuming the agreement removes all conditions.',['Context: missing documentation knowledge and available support.','Reason: match capability to the remaining access barrier.'])}
P={'P01':(['01-00','01-02'],'State (a) one distinction between SACU and SADC [2]. Explain (b) why fictional tariff cooperation without a functioning bridge may not deliver the intended regional market gains [4].','(a) SACU is a customs union with common customs arrangements; SADC has a broader regional development and integration scope. (b) Reduced trade charges may improve incentives, but the physical connection still prevents deliveries; complementary implementation is needed to turn the agreement into usable market access.',['Point: two correctly paired institutional features [2].','Development: link tariff opportunity and physical constraint to the practical result [4].']),
'P02':(['01-00','02-00'],'Describe (a) this fictional comparison under similar trade terms: A’s deliveries take eight days and 30% arrive late; B’s take three days and 5% late [3]. Suggest (b) a priority for A if unreliable road access is documented and the aim is dependable sales [3].','(a) A combines five more delivery days with a late share 25 points higher. (b) Address the documented road reliability with responsible providers and maintenance, then monitor delivery; identical agreement terms do not resolve unequal practical access.',['Evidence: paired time and late-share comparisons [3].','Meaning: match an implementable priority to the actual barrier and aim [3].'])}
examples=[('01','Agreement and implementation',Q[1][0]+' '+Q[1][1],['01-00','01-01','01-02']),('02','Market access requires capabilities',Q[5][0]+' '+Q[5][1],['02-00']),('01','Official institutional references','Roles and membership checked 20 October 2026 using [SACU](https://www.sacu.int/about-sacu), its [trade-facilitation functions](https://www.sacu.int/trade-facilitation-and-revenue-management) and [SADC objectives](https://sadc.int/pages/sadc-objectives). Shared revenue and integration are institutional arrangements; the fictional cases are not official performance assessments.',['01-00','01-01','01-02']),('02','Cooperation source','The [EU Council’s trade-agreement overview](https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/policies/trade-agreements/), checked 20 October 2026, describes trade partnerships including development-related agreements. Specific tariffs, product rules and eligibility require the relevant current official agreement; this lesson does not invent them.',['02-00'])]
